#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068586 :question/id #uuid "fce14198-c8ee-50a6-981d-85df79cce761" :question/math-academy-id "e-507" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051032 :topic/math-academy-id 382 :topic/title "Factoring Perfect Square Trinomials With Leading Coefficients"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35cdedaa-2971-5cd3-b239-0423a71c6b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{36})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330429} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc8531b-20c3-5c50-9e7c-90adbbc4c609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{24})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330919} {:answer/id #uuid "7731bb28-1882-5e20-a675-fbdb1096d84c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(168)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331015} {:answer/id #uuid "859367df-628b-54a6-9551-215ec16a1e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331135} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc56717-5b83-578f-a99a-4c6c6035ec8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{32})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bc8531b-20c3-5c50-9e7c-90adbbc4c609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{24})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330919} :answer-field/id #uuid "f092496f-4167-57cc-ad3a-f1e3a758daf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332624}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b7a0d53-d8cd-5e6c-b6e9-1877172e8d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-49761" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16=(x+4)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16} & =\\frac{3x+4}{(x+4)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{(x+4)}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n3x+4=A(x-4)+B(x+4).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4)+4 & =A(4-4)+B(4+4) \\\\ 16 & =8B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-4)+4 & =A(-4-4)+B(-4+4) \\\\ -8 & =-8A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}=\\frac{1}{(x+4)}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(x+4)}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-4)^{2}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)(x-4)^{2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3x+4}{{x}^{2}-16}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)(x-4)^{2}|{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(24) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{24}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8acf67b3-93bf-549d-9c50-26f933c82ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067469} {:answer/id #uuid "8c45cb13-4e05-5bff-a7a0-561fd848029d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{20}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067470} {:answer/id #uuid "bf27595e-98fb-50de-940d-997dd079e3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067471} {:answer/id #uuid "f715cd54-b9c2-5d74-88de-1757cf436473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{32}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067472} {:answer/id #uuid "114a2306-66da-5c82-ab19-ae946b637713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{16}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067473}] :answer-field/id #uuid "64c419c7-a91a-581a-b296-3a8356a41f2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067468}] :question/id #uuid "25dcfa81-6d0a-5f2d-9d07-6a9bf3dcb175" :question/math-academy-id "q-49766" :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{4}^{7}(2x + 2)/((x - 3)(x + 5))dx$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f8b9bdb-9838-55d0-bb49-abab662dbde1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{2}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f8b9bdb-9838-55d0-bb49-abab662dbde1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{2}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234769} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc37ec3a-8597-5c61-a04b-d9684c51116f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235222}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f9f3c49-fab7-5715-8827-fab0d52bd33d" :question/math-academy-id "q-128238" :question/problem "Find the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can integrate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C & =\\frac{1}{2}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3{|}^{5}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1{|}^{3})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x+3)^{5}}{(x+1)^{3}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x+3)^{5}}{(x+1)^{3}}|]}_{-5}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{4}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{4}^{3}}{{2}^{5}\\cdot {3}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e62c0ae-d8d1-54c4-b59e-37ff1225c3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{40}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e62c0ae-d8d1-54c4-b59e-37ff1225c3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{40}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317998} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b15ad3-db26-57a4-ae32-bd15d4341f5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318783}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fc7feda-9975-5f67-aaa7-81f6387a8c6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-347077" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)} & =\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+2=A(x+4)+B(x-2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-4)+2 & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-2) \\\\ -6 & =-6B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+2 & =A(2+4)+B(2-2) \\\\ 6 & =6A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}=\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{x+4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)(x+4)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)(x+4)|{|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{40}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5138ba05-d746-5a38-93a2-145c4ae9c17d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5138ba05-d746-5a38-93a2-145c4ae9c17d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317834} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d43e545-60f4-5e7f-a28d-5ee9daafa778" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318735}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6b13617e-d037-5962-9e92-2f6869856914" :question/math-academy-id "q-348975" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+12=(x-2)(x-6).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12} & =\\frac{3x+7}{(x-2)(x-6)}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{x-6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n3x+7=A(x-6)+B(x-2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(6)+7 & =A(6-6)+B(6-2) \\\\ 25 & =4B \\\\ B & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)+7 & =A(2-6)+B(2-2) \\\\ 13 & =-4A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}=-\\frac{13}{4(x-2)}+\\frac{25}{4(x-6)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{13}{4(x-2)}+\\frac{25}{4(x-6)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}-8x+12}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))-(-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-\\frac{25}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bba51c12-d21a-503a-bc79-ad303e35736c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba51c12-d21a-503a-bc79-ad303e35736c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318189} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa39d523-3134-5de7-a3c6-37f18e45f848" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318947}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "87498ed9-b2f1-5890-a6bb-4cd539bb2116" :question/math-academy-id "q-348976" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x-5=(x+5)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5} & =\\frac{5x-1}{(x+5)(x-1)}=\\frac{A}{x+5}+\\frac{B}{x-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x-1=A(x-1)+B(x+5).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)-1 & =A(1-1)+B(1+5) \\\\ 4 & =6B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-5,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-5)-1 & =A(-5-1)+B(-5+5) \\\\ -26 & =-6A \\\\ A & =\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}=\\frac{13}{3(x+5)}+\\frac{2}{3(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{13}{3(x+5)}+\\frac{2}{3(x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|]}_{-4}^{0} \\\\  & =(\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))-(\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ec98abd-fe65-520a-8a2e-4e7d1cfc5d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{49}{48})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ec98abd-fe65-520a-8a2e-4e7d1cfc5d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{49}{48})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317950} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af6759a-4e3b-540c-bb7b-e531ae752551" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318589}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1545917f-fc9b-5e7b-a9c9-f1def306bc53" :question/math-academy-id "q-349040" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n12{x}^{2}-x-6=(4x-3)(3x+2).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\frac{36x-10}{(4x-3)(3x+2)}=\\frac{A}{4x-3}+\\frac{B}{3x+2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n36x-10=A(3x+2)+B(4x-3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{4},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(\\frac{3}{4})-10 & =A(3(\\frac{3}{4})+2)+B(4(\\frac{3}{4})-3) \\\\ 17 & =\\frac{17}{4}A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(-\\frac{2}{3})-10 & =A(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2)+B(4(-\\frac{2}{3})-3) \\\\ -34 & =-\\frac{17}{3}B \\\\ B & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}=\\frac{4}{4x-3}+\\frac{6}{3x+2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{4x-3}+\\frac{6}{3x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-3|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-3|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(4x-3)(3x+2)^{2}|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(4x-3)(3x+2)^{2}|{|}_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{48}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{48}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb6eb457-d510-5aac-a140-c028efa83fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{121}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb6eb457-d510-5aac-a140-c028efa83fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{121}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318239} :answer-field/id #uuid "080179ad-a368-58bf-b1b3-7a9fa9c592e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318443}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0a37242-8ca3-5cb0-9c32-a06114fb7f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-349041" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-19x-4=(5x+1)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4} & =\\frac{15x-39}{(5x+1)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{5x+1}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n15x-39=A(x-4)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(4)-39 & =A(4-4)+B(5(4)+1) \\\\ 21 & =21B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(-\\frac{1}{5})-39 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}-4)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ -42 & =-\\frac{21}{5}A \\\\ A & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}=\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(242)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{242}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{121}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3650f196-0328-5530-9753-b8daea34bdea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330436} {:answer/id #uuid "61a1c243-0a40-5e5e-8182-6b4e260f7c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330819} {:answer/id #uuid "61b21e1e-34a9-5d15-8dad-b9c6a8ae79c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330823} {:answer/id #uuid "b43186d3-f02f-528f-95bb-132abdb4a929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331543} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3aeb4a-cf96-5583-932f-e7dc699836f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61b21e1e-34a9-5d15-8dad-b9c6a8ae79c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330823} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf6d8557-cd9c-50f2-aeed-85b67a88fc2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1a17000-df27-56c4-97fa-86fb2f031e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-49762" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4=(x-2)(x+2).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{2x+6}{(x-2)(x+2)}=\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+6=A(x-2)+B(x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+6 & =A(2-2)+B(2+2) \\\\ 10 & =4B \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+6 & =A(-2-2)+B(-2+2) \\\\ 2 & =-4A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}=-\\frac{1}{2(x+2)}+\\frac{5}{2(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2(x+2)}+\\frac{5}{2(x-2)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)^{5}|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)^{5}|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{x+2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{x+2}|{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-\\frac{1}{3}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-{3}^{5}|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{-1})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193544b0-a900-5bf2-a458-123a5bb0d8c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330163} {:answer/id #uuid "441e48ce-8167-57ce-8725-b4dbc3e6d6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330559} {:answer/id #uuid "9854d0c4-92e3-5c35-8435-1a532583bad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331308} {:answer/id #uuid "ac14f538-b8bb-570e-9678-3ec6bfecdb81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331478} {:answer/id #uuid "e89843a7-ad1b-5b3f-92f5-5ade0d0b5286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9854d0c4-92e3-5c35-8435-1a532583bad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331308} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5ef8dd-65ae-5b37-9e36-b7ee665d0cb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332429}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "502d82d3-a5f7-508f-bf84-492789902473" :question/math-academy-id "q-49764" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}=\\frac{A}{(x+7)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x+29=A(x+4)+B(x+7).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4)+29 & =A(-4+4)+B(-4+7) \\\\ 9 & =3B \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-7,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-7)+29 & =A(-7+4)+B(-7+7) \\\\ -6 & =-3A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}=\\frac{2}{(x+7)}+\\frac{3}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x+7}+\\frac{3}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x+7}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+7|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)^{3}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)^{3}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|{|}_{-6}^{-5} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "063c6875-b172-57ea-9516-b472ac204607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330007} {:answer/id #uuid "6107bdfd-103d-53b2-a033-60f300bafef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330810} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e59150-73db-57fd-b819-1de200e3af72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331540} {:answer/id #uuid "ca68051f-97f6-5c33-b759-045dfea597be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331743} {:answer/id #uuid "f44c9159-3dc6-52a7-ae24-2f74136d8911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca68051f-97f6-5c33-b759-045dfea597be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331743} :answer-field/id #uuid "771c09f3-b8e5-5ae0-87d1-2ce9dbf7c79c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332415}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a39ee30d-e827-513c-ac47-60805a357d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-49765" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-7}^{-3}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)} & =\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x+8)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n11x+46=A(x+8)+B(x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-8,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11(-8)+46 & =A(-8+8)+B(-8+2) \\\\ -42 & =-6B \\\\ B & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11(-2)+46 & =A(-2+8)+B(-2+2) \\\\ 24 & =6A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)}+\\frac{7}{(x+8)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{x+2}+\\frac{7}{x+8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{x+8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n            \n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{4}(x+8)^{7}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-7}^{-3}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{4}(x+8)^{7}|{|}_{-7}^{-3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{7})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{4}) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4337e381-2a60-5c5c-9308-276258a90336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330554} {:answer/id #uuid "639dd384-bc8a-5ba4-914e-7b3e50785991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330837} {:answer/id #uuid "85f0701b-e023-56df-926f-7a39fc270845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331139} {:answer/id #uuid "a9341903-3233-5c3a-89b3-545d494844f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331451} {:answer/id #uuid "afc09428-01ed-5d60-b345-93b553f0092c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9341903-3233-5c3a-89b3-545d494844f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331451} :answer-field/id #uuid "d06cfba7-b789-5962-80c5-2ed56e573863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332563}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74490665-fc5e-5bc9-a392-77f2142c3626" :question/math-academy-id "q-49769" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx+1=A(x+3)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+1 & =A(-3+3)+B(-3) \\\\ -2 & =-3B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+1 & =A(0+3)+B(0) \\\\ 1 & =3A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "151da108-2d1a-5bc5-b41f-3452f296556f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342423} {:answer/id #uuid "6541d20f-1ff9-546c-9c03-a8ef80814ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343097} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b6e00e-897a-5d95-a121-a2ba216a3005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343797} {:answer/id #uuid "c2432a49-d9c3-5f08-881b-95bc366e4923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343944} {:answer/id #uuid "eecb1e2a-7344-5ba9-8e2a-6cca8c0e342f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1b6e00e-897a-5d95-a121-a2ba216a3005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343797} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bdd9cde-ccbc-562f-bc45-0c204929d46a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344518}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "33fe88ae-755e-5444-9db1-f4813a03f297" :question/math-academy-id "q-75825" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x-8=(x+2)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8} & =\\frac{3x-6}{(x+2)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n3x-6=A(x-4)+B(x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4)-6 & =A(4-4)+B(4+2) \\\\ 6 & =6B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2)-6 & =A(-2-4)+B(-2+2) \\\\ -12 & =-6A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}=\\frac{2}{x+2}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x+2}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{2}(x-4)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{2}(x-4)|{|}_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241e4e41-3877-590f-b9fb-f9441f5491c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342541} {:answer/id #uuid "2a868b11-a8ff-5adf-ab9a-9bf43adf6226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342591} {:answer/id #uuid "8689e7c8-b5a6-587c-8850-666b874ed56a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343396} {:answer/id #uuid "d624b7cc-f612-5da1-90f6-60de0f22b6c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{8}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344113} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7baec1-72af-559b-b4c9-4860c48320be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(28)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8689e7c8-b5a6-587c-8850-666b874ed56a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343396} :answer-field/id #uuid "db324572-0ff6-5834-9630-8030d14c66f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344872}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36f60655-da6b-51ba-a53b-de029dead882" :question/math-academy-id "q-75828" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+16x+3=(5x+1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3} & =\\frac{14}{(5x+1)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{(5x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+3)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n14=A(x+3)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-3+3)+B(5(-3)+1) \\\\ 14 & =-14B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}+3)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{14}{5}A \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}=\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|{|}_{4}^{11} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083195 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083001 :question/id #uuid "14b4c34b-bbb0-5a1a-b987-b2a60f0e9fc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-511" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac { 8}{t^5}  \\,\\textrm{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-4}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515 :topic/math-academy-id 1905 :topic/title "Limits of Reciprocal Functions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2746f869-b08f-539f-ba4c-a9b25a44fec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272733} {:answer/id #uuid "67c5903f-fdc0-5c26-82c1-b1ec98c3f91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273283} {:answer/id #uuid "78eab62d-b9dd-5d08-9874-5d64c4d44684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273423} {:answer/id #uuid "792913e9-c154-597b-9079-6904512897c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273428} {:answer/id #uuid "a733beca-2cd8-557e-9b1e-b679dc6f45bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67c5903f-fdc0-5c26-82c1-b1ec98c3f91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273283} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cd1bbed-de89-588c-ba9d-2938ed458f6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274819}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b3895ee-aa12-5868-a13a-ac9c31fb2e63" :question/math-academy-id "q-152777" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{x}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{7}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{7}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{7}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e227908-fbbc-5b4a-8f42-0cf97d435896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330661} {:answer/id #uuid "6b518463-9db4-5b6d-8d6f-b4ca49edc399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330915} {:answer/id #uuid "6f15e05a-59f6-5850-8e78-27d40efca2c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330949} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3adecf-b533-5eaa-b404-216cf5351cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331334} {:answer/id #uuid "de098ac5-694b-5b16-8558-81ba1c162b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e227908-fbbc-5b4a-8f42-0cf97d435896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330661} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a3d8b9b-2b58-5c5a-b819-9cd644ee883d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332327}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7e5736b-250b-5f2d-96b7-c89915cce3dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-5962" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{\\theta }^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{\\theta }^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{\\theta }^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}3{\\theta }^{-3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{3}{2}{\\theta }^{-2})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2f49d51-a569-54b6-8499-614594a99518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(9/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2f49d51-a569-54b6-8499-614594a99518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(9/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234902} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb66c969-a116-55b7-83c4-7bdad75642f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235274}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "687b0f0d-b7fc-5c94-b602-200dc9ae2630" :question/math-academy-id "q-128340" :question/problem "${\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the indefinite integral first. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the improper integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{a-3}{2a+1}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{9}|) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1-\\frac{3}{a}}{2+\\frac{1}{a}}|)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{9}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "211c0aa1-6235-5917-a6e1-5024b33bacec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272681} {:answer/id #uuid "b2166127-ed32-5ae9-8e8b-999121c80ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273924} {:answer/id #uuid "c0875150-b445-569b-9956-6c8ff9a35327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274056} {:answer/id #uuid "d33934d0-d9e3-5e37-8cf8-5f828c042d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274238} {:answer/id #uuid "e1628935-ffab-572a-8b2d-f55a0936921e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0875150-b445-569b-9956-6c8ff9a35327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274056} :answer-field/id #uuid "961ca5f0-33bb-5a8f-95eb-fdc37419bba0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274894}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce005079-2956-5000-9ad8-11b835eb244d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152755" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}2{\\theta }^{-6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{2}{5}{\\theta }^{-5})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{5}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "577b581c-5e2c-5183-82c6-db5e22ae1bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273137} {:answer/id #uuid "5e91ad5b-f000-53f8-b2f5-8126ba775b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273201} {:answer/id #uuid "7b00012e-e262-59a8-905e-0981695eaa20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273442} {:answer/id #uuid "840314ee-0921-5c6e-b9b6-13daad9caebe" :answer/type {:db/i