academy-id "q-350331" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}y & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{y}^{-8/3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{5}{y}^{-5/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{5}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{5}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{5}}}) \\\\  & =-3(0-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf7372ff-a95c-5140-9146-b9e95e23882d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf7372ff-a95c-5140-9146-b9e95e23882d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318253} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a052466-0020-5268-9c1b-c0202f71b6a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318763}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "039703b5-2c46-51fb-a40a-a73595491778" :question/math-academy-id "q-350333" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}s=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =12{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{s}^{-7/3}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =12{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{4}{s}^{-4/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{4}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{4}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{4}}}) \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4405f0b5-3014-5db5-b9e0-baacfc0fc223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4405f0b5-3014-5db5-b9e0-baacfc0fc223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317785} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f753ad3-4c9c-5606-8519-a99985830b59" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318488}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0e61ba4-3fcc-5778-93a8-ad7c6742d93e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350337" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[5]{{t}^{13}}}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[5]{{t}^{13}}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[5]{{t}^{13}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{t}^{-13/5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{5}{8}{t}^{-8/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{t}^{8}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{a}^{8}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{1}^{8}}}) \\\\  & =-5(0-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f55c7229-8db4-5c89-a24b-f4a1c56dbe68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368964} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd9b88b-f6f3-593c-8fe2-f8e2602b449e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368965} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1b3775-48c8-5874-b612-f7fb378b4ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368966} {:answer/id #uuid "6237fde9-2a98-5b89-b3f1-c5ea22316452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368967} {:answer/id #uuid "08628f72-2d8b-5346-961d-52158626cee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fd9b88b-f6f3-593c-8fe2-f8e2602b449e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368965} :answer-field/id #uuid "55b2b187-cd84-5430-b2a4-819a4d4afae0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368963}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61d50393-796e-587b-99a7-a3d3d840da4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100310" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}}\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}}\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{4}{z})]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{4}{a}+\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17054bf5-52f5-56f4-9a5d-2be749a6e48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369834} {:answer/id #uuid "0aa94db3-9838-5c69-b479-b6f20018374d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369835} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2956d9-8c80-503d-9229-cae165e70be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369836} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8a7946-143f-591d-8adc-2cd8b5beea49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369837} {:answer/id #uuid "3a16084d-2b2d-5b1a-b8b5-8ec16f0f7978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17054bf5-52f5-56f4-9a5d-2be749a6e48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369834} :answer-field/id #uuid "44437d5c-4b57-5518-9706-0e146b8ec3fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369833}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51474c0d-3481-5d2e-9a70-04762078286c" :question/math-academy-id "q-152754" :question/problem "${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{t}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab51b940-9e47-5c28-ab31-cf2045c87ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab51b940-9e47-5c28-ab31-cf2045c87ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370629} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5dc3c8-4f89-52e4-9fbf-7b1dcf21fbf9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370628}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1aa4c968-db06-5f2f-94f2-fe8dae20562b" :question/math-academy-id "q-350324" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =16{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =16{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-2})]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-8(0-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083201 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083003 :question/id #uuid "714c93f9-6170-52a9-849b-2e9d94805c4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-513" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x} }\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[2{x}^{1/2}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\sqrt{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{b}-1] \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\n- For the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{1+x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{1-2}+\\frac{1}{1+a}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{1+a}+1] \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\n- For the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048435 :topic/math-academy-id 1986 :topic/title "Limits of Radical Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050490 :topic/math-academy-id 1377 :topic/title "Limits of Logarithmic Functions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23acf82b-e58d-58e8-851e-1bcf8b34a403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460107} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6485ad-62f1-5cc1-845b-f8acead5d2d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf6485ad-62f1-5cc1-845b-f8acead5d2d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460108} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc3c2e9c-4064-5705-bb22-4da95d61f120" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f30616f-ab91-58ef-a4d5-ee0fe6b58990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460109} {:answer/id #uuid "e7418585-e683-5057-9c73-a1c0c5846fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7418585-e683-5057-9c73-a1c0c5846fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460110} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c3c2d32-ecdd-580f-886b-8931da49ff10" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460921} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026ffc31-daed-530c-b15b-74e8ab85c121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460111} {:answer/id #uuid "e220e77a-c7e1-51e6-b067-3d2066d61dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "026ffc31-daed-530c-b15b-74e8ab85c121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460111} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d0ef17-3ba8-5540-9bf6-1a11258717fe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460922}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "998df1b1-461f-5fc4-87ac-c188c53c68e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-6032" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{10}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-1}]}_{10}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2\\sqrt{10-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-6) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|) \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd9b6d3c-0057-574c-87f0-e4101749ea2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377616} {:answer/id #uuid "3737c8d2-a0da-5074-ba6f-697e878e8a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377617} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c32b1a-b4cd-59d4-99ae-00c65bacae91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377618} {:answer/id #uuid "d2acd056-6854-53f2-9f3f-d23ce96038ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377619} {:answer/id #uuid "1eac7a83-db29-5df2-847e-ba35f30a046a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2c32b1a-b4cd-59d4-99ae-00c65bacae91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377618} :answer-field/id #uuid "88a03e6d-8814-51d2-8edb-239663fe528e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377615}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "322fb4b3-6749-5356-82bf-9541f4f0d3ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-6294" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2a+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2a+1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21b7bac2-7c49-5bca-b0a4-69dc3a8c9bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236035} {:answer/id #uuid "440f0c7e-4732-5a58-8653-34d1d6a4c23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236255} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d494d7-8ebd-5802-8686-db43d5ff7fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236949} {:answer/id #uuid "c46ac0dc-03a9-5a1a-8f79-a9bea31a3de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237059} {:answer/id #uuid "dac1ee25-c1c3-53e3-b4cf-2ad6e22e94c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "440f0c7e-4732-5a58-8653-34d1d6a4c23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236255} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1733310-60f1-5249-b1c5-9809a13bbc72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237686}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "702f5890-f962-5297-b8db-429117ade5e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-100642" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{3}{2}(2z+1)^{2/3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(2z+1)^{2/3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }((2a+1)^{2/3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c33a18d-104d-52e9-9c2f-5e801a820664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235881} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8d46d3-212e-5d20-a7db-1772936c7f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236096} {:answer/id #uuid "3e50bf74-aff8-5e86-9467-5aa0194a6d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236209} {:answer/id #uuid "468b0303-942b-5eae-80d1-6f45657de69f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236271} {:answer/id #uuid "de9f84f3-7d6b-52fd-87cc-c8e2ef3fd885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c33a18d-104d-52e9-9c2f-5e801a820664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235881} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5392877-e39b-5b6d-8019-6b4895e6d441" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237757}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8508ef3-0736-5531-a2ca-192705b437b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-100643" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2-3t}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{2+3}-\\sqrt{2-3a}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{2-3a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff5263a7-1895-5085-8df6-00b5e700d455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461171} {:answer/id #uuid "6a138658-7727-5b84-9147-e75c4bc6f6be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff5263a7-1895-5085-8df6-00b5e700d455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461171} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fe9a7c8-6e85-594b-b112-88855ef364c3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462339} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a49961ff-3ffb-57ca-a127-a6af05c42338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461173} {:answer/id #uuid "283b72c8-abd2-59aa-94d2-c526caba130c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a49961ff-3ffb-57ca-a127-a6af05c42338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461173} :answer-field/id #uuid "de28bf36-f83a-5fcb-b309-eca4f69c6915" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462340} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a089b8d-46c8-5953-a6f9-b2962253f826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461175} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3d8c52-3f24-5cde-b9fc-ebd2788b23f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3d8c52-3f24-5cde-b9fc-ebd2788b23f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461176} :answer-field/id #uuid "292bf9b3-4412-5dd3-80d1-271235a3924d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462341}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e90080f-3ef7-5c8d-a0d7-de79fffa1eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-101735" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{z+3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a+3}+\\frac{1}{0+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals III is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11cbf150-b683-51fa-9f8a-d203bcfb3ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461177} {:answer/id #uuid "5956e27d-8d17-501a-8856-f798cc05f016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11cbf150-b683-51fa-9f8a-d203bcfb3ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461177} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c8fa3d2-c067-5b43-a12b-85e271c9cb1e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462342} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4de3cb94-3a2c-5891-aae8-6ea29f0d4df4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461179} {:answer/id #uuid "8565509e-ebc2-5ae6-96ae-9d8817a43af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8565509e-ebc2-5ae6-96ae-9d8817a43af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461180} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f996994-3b27-587f-9d4f-5527cd7a6f32" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70ebaee1-04f7-54b6-af95-172ae288615f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461181} {:answer/id #uuid "4626a4f4-a591-579d-a987-b3940f00bbae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4626a4f4-a591-579d-a987-b3940f00bbae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461182} :answer-field/id #uuid "81f2e71e-76d6-53a1-bb19-e51bae15960a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462344}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30834ba5-7de3-5c58-8e09-a32166c98581" :question/math-academy-id "q-101738" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u-1}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a-1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(0-{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[4\\sqrt{x-2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-\\sqrt{3-2}) \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\nIn conclusion, only integrals II and III are divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3447b210-752f-58bc-ae21-6727e68e220b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285271} {:answer/id #uuid "68cdc6da-3b7a-57fc-8b9f-cd3018eea1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285751} {:answer/id #uuid "6d97721e-a156-5007-a747-5f849a77796c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285798} {:answer/id #uuid "888fbc31-5edc-5fc6-ba9c-9dca4d6a73b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286040} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4d35d0-edf6-5b04-974a-d41a0d358490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3447b210-752f-58bc-ae21-6727e68e220b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285271} :answer-field/id #uuid "43979b98-c797-55a1-8e56-30f10ae875af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287297}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c15a1f53-63be-5a09-b32e-0a9e0c9c95b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-20700" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 2 & \\infty  \\\\ u & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)] \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0864cdf9-1ffd-56c1-8cec-9c7e3634c2e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330021} {:answer/id #uuid "757f2c97-4c6e-5a4b-b58e-740ddec39baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331005} {:answer/id #uuid "c02ca5ed-e5be-530f-b339-6203cf61077f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331655} {:answer/id #uuid "d82819b4-466e-564f-a4fd-bc1c368333eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331867} {:answer/id #uuid "e39d5946-f20c-5fd9-9c4f-e13376e2ce60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0864cdf9-1ffd-56c1-8cec-9c7e3634c2e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330021} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b6c3b91-0868-5cbe-bd0f-5ddc36c6f516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332333}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06fd8b41-8200-5edd-a18c-2621495e1f13" :question/math-academy-id "q-5964" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{4/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{4/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{4/5}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36b26abc-5