86348367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13276adc-5e19-53a0-9f9a-51eb15d41b59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345516} {:answer/id #uuid "23ba0748-be66-5c10-9740-7a5fc964cba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345679} {:answer/id #uuid "705951cd-43f5-516c-9a5f-baf621993227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346332} {:answer/id #uuid "e20fc376-1370-5720-9da8-c277fecbffc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347337} {:answer/id #uuid "f17275a5-42e9-5faa-a693-6409875653d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23ba0748-be66-5c10-9740-7a5fc964cba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345679} :answer-field/id #uuid "20fa39a2-e338-57a1-ae53-4506e3856b9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347661}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "027c33f1-e979-5d6a-94a5-ba6a2c9a56e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-80342" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-2\\sqrt{1-z}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{1-0}-\\sqrt{1-a}) \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{1-a} \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cae36fd-3deb-5b7b-8b36-9f9f8dfee127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345599} {:answer/id #uuid "583455da-d6e1-5029-a452-b73277cbbf12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346105} {:answer/id #uuid "613d7756-c42b-5e58-8499-7e22134bae89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346194} {:answer/id #uuid "7b39e153-c534-5ace-84e4-fe8c0f3ea308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346428} {:answer/id #uuid "a987b942-fa10-5bee-b0d4-7db1e3099b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a987b942-fa10-5bee-b0d4-7db1e3099b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346830} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fc125c1-0b75-5549-98a7-4582698ca44e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347776}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc51916f-5f62-552c-9209-e6b4fd87da10" :question/math-academy-id "q-80347" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=8-{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=8-{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & -\\infty  \\\\ u & 9 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\infty }^{9}(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{u}})\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{9}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{9}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{u}]}_{9}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{9}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc0f0bbf-9e98-54b3-bf3e-86d3585281f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468852} {:answer/id #uuid "0364d1e1-fd92-5dce-b6af-859298fcbb61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0364d1e1-fd92-5dce-b6af-859298fcbb61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468853} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a22fa4-849c-5c90-9822-62f2fcf23aaf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470038} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308906a6-4c6a-54ce-a9d9-4762d925306b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468854} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f5d776-8ae6-5de7-96df-59aa6fc9e843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4f5d776-8ae6-5de7-96df-59aa6fc9e843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468855} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cdd4872-c6a0-59bf-8e3c-aadb70e9ae28" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470039} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1935d638-cd1f-5a78-8128-5b81e02f9c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468856} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5e324c-feff-5279-9a68-2ee89b03c35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1935d638-cd1f-5a78-8128-5b81e02f9c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b36b9451-aa6f-5293-a01d-78f77c5a1f3f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470040}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebe5f91b-e612-5062-b7df-947a793c33b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-101734" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-5}]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2\\sqrt{6-5}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{1/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt[3]{a}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4a6913-c932-5739-8608-9cc06b956797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369966} {:answer/id #uuid "5b620ce7-5cea-5d5e-b6ee-c0c245ff836e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369967} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6badc9-86fe-508a-9845-ba404048d557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369968} {:answer/id #uuid "1802544e-90f5-58a5-97c6-5c3fad71b6e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369969} {:answer/id #uuid "b1446da5-03f2-5f98-8109-b170d5293fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd4a6913-c932-5739-8608-9cc06b956797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369966} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5cb1ae6-80cc-5989-a4fa-22e6a9f2c9c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369965}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94bad8ac-08e4-5bfe-8807-f8590eb5dbd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-20698" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3a+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3a+1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afdb2771-62f0-54fa-9623-4972845d19a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470643} {:answer/id #uuid "29258b68-aef8-521d-bcd0-4ced0913a87b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afdb2771-62f0-54fa-9623-4972845d19a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470643} :answer-field/id #uuid "84edb204-5bb6-5b15-bce3-ab37bb9167a4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471827} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "671dc28a-4f0a-5152-a704-c6f85c3291ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470645} {:answer/id #uuid "86897aab-f9f4-534b-a305-6d708d258191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86897aab-f9f4-534b-a305-6d708d258191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470646} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa7980c7-726d-5b91-94fd-76106f224eca" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471828} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a0d9e7-b5e7-588d-816d-4ab61e18e992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470647} {:answer/id #uuid "e352800d-a7f4-56b9-b333-aa4cd4f14a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16a0d9e7-b5e7-588d-816d-4ab61e18e992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470647} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1f5d5e-e005-55bf-8ec5-9de9792267ea" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471829}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79e7ca1d-1ed8-5888-8523-0ec8ed90db1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-101736" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z$\nIII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2(u-1)^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2(2-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{z}^{3}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integral II is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a656c4-82b8-5e88-8816-0dc0a192bb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481753} {:answer/id #uuid "80096874-46a7-55a9-a918-5e3243f639ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80096874-46a7-55a9-a918-5e3243f639ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481758} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c3cc50-8f49-5f0a-a0d2-109c1fb2b62f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481755} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145423fa-33a3-52aa-ae1d-b24235875c7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481754} {:answer/id #uuid "3630167e-491c-546f-ac6e-210342635c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3630167e-491c-546f-ac6e-210342635c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481756} :answer-field/id #uuid "9525a6e4-2431-55ce-91b6-40d7a688e4cf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "775f0f81-df07-5d86-9ff9-7f28b4ccaf9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481757} {:answer/id #uuid "d1da4748-78a9-5547-93fa-277269ba94ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1da4748-78a9-5547-93fa-277269ba94ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481761} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5ca4e79-9a78-5e09-ad2e-1b4358963f5c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481760}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e50acc57-5eee-5203-9dd8-5e7531f41fa1" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-1}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2\\sqrt{2-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|) \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083344 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082895 :question/id #uuid "54852305-01f3-5036-a31c-d7ddcfd7c100" :question/math-academy-id "e-10717" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332 :topic/math-academy-id 1159 :topic/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5844549f-b897-5a58-bf08-acfbe8efa2a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251276} {:answer/id #uuid "7667e2f4-ee83-5482-af7c-d2bdd84fc202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251526} {:answer/id #uuid "a218e653-d88f-5440-be12-f0ad6810fe89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251949} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cc8e9f-2139-5926-aacc-b95747012288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252321} {:answer/id #uuid "f79ecc31-c3ea-5c7d-ae4d-ea007b99bc9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7667e2f4-ee83-5482-af7c-d2bdd84fc202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251526} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f22f9c0-c715-5c9f-b358-40ff3aa9350f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253084}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d988115-5b28-5cc4-948a-8ebc728e32c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118619" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+{x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[-{u}^{-1}]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{a}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc22d002-389e-5aff-af28-e8e6f5eb7e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081960} {:answer/id #uuid "30e34f8a-b0af-58be-913e-6d9fb2663d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081961} {:answer/id #uuid "3b073e75-824d-5cb5-b2e0-9f4d981135b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081962} {:answer/id #uuid "6140438b-2a65-5a01-86b7-bd9d34ada694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081963} {:answer/id #uuid "62837da9-fd79-57dd-9cf6-0181318bc6b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081964}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ea46c59b-0458-5284-8965-fc4166ae4490" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081959}] :question/id #uuid "5f0f6992-34a7-57de-832a-457759136b12" :question/math-academy-id "q-6297" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}(1)/(\\sqrt{(x + 4)^{5}})dx$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2796c11a-4fa6-505d-9e0a-aa25efdb9c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250892} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec32c39-76e1-531c-a980-52d6069a233f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251335} {:answer/id #uuid "946eb912-f177-51c6-8055-52cb6bf80ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251830} {:answer/id #uuid "9c077374-79f0-523e-825a-f5521b2be909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251898} {:answer/id #uuid "bd295589-d9fe-5cee-b9fd-e8e6c502b237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ec32c39-76e1-531c-a980-52d6069a233f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251335} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8899cbd-a6dc-541a-a768-564111ff4288" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253151}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bfa67a4d-773b-5a56-87ac-2e520036ce73" :question/math-academy-id "q-118618" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b40290-f4b5-5a0c-be7c-96b2f991be33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272884} {:answer/id #uuid "58e3e781-1f60-5931-b1ab-d40d34456c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273147} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcef9fb-9792-5a33-9834-01011e4cecef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274179} {:answer/id #uuid "d21993ef-a070-5549-8573-0bebbc8c466f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274222} {:answer/id #uuid "fa381c2a-d88b-56af-ae05-33eda16e16c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58e3e781-1f60-5931-b1ab-d40d34456c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273147} :answer-field/id #uuid "00ce7354-3030-510c-bcbd-23e31ec43dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274637}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9866e046-eb23-5536-9118-9338cb3fa801" :question/math-academy-id "q-152045" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2x+4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x+4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a11a46-1ee3-5e97-8f5c-d2abb45897e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272726} {:answer/id #uuid "3ac4135a-ba42-51f2-9589-6f253f1dccac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272898} {:answer/id #uuid "3d241674-25f3-586b-9f92-1ae97b73f2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272911} {:answer/id #uuid "82c45777-32c0-5bbe-903e-12a9832686d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273509} {:answer/id #uuid "e43e2450-5746-5333-88fb-b2698cd7c90f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d241674-25f3-586b-9f92-1ae97b73f2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272911} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9379364-a06d-5360-b86f-2168fc1e5c00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275074}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b4ebe1c-7aff-5381-8ad5-281043ebfb60" :question/math-academy-id "q-152056" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+1)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+1)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{-3/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{3}}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c25cd7-e43c-5867-9197-1ea57513487c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272470} {:answer/id #uuid "aa37f140-928a-5f9c-98df-08319686b282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273858} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4f57fe-edf1-5a1a-80e8-43a57330b833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274040} {:answer/id #uuid "c27f9c75-c61b-5146-a432-d5051ff1cbbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274074} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fe9d4f-ca4d-59b2-bf98-68697411b047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa37f140-928a-5f9c-98df-08319686b282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273858} :answer-field/id #uuid "826506dc-d5aa-52cf-8357-625d503add96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274858}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69ccaa61-239d-507f-a5cc-30c609c287b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-152779" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{(z+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=z+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $z\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=z+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $z$ | $-1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{(z+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{(z+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32496a0b-0230-56b7-a08f-0eacec67f0e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272838} {:answer/id #uuid "54d16173-4764-586a-9e7d-eae609ca54b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The Integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273110} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3a8ce3-a04f-549b-961f-7f4308c92010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273346} {:answer/id #uuid "cff5572a-a2b4-5582-bb9e-a320f544f89d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274200} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4ae8a0-1bfb-5e1e-a786-8e51d30074fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e3a8ce3-a04f-549b-961f-7f4308c92010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273346} :answer-field/id #uuid "3032f180-f025-53e0-856a-88e12dd4d4d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274720}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12c7271a-e53c-5579-a182-e59f266fc9f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-152785" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{z}^{3}}{{({z}^{4}+1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={z}^{4}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=4{z}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u={z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $z\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={z}^{4}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $z$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{z}^{3}}{{({z}^{4}+1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{u}^{4}}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{z}^{3}}{{({z}^{4}+1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b84759-63fa-5697-9589-8a03b63ecdef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2b84759-63fa-5697-9589-8a03b63ecdef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318372} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b766f3-690f-5bb1-b9b8-11c48d9b7aed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318815}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ad09e6e-7d4e-5f78-9ad8-d40330d32874" :question/math-academy-id "q-350527" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }3{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}3{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{4}{u}^{-4}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-4}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30bb13ff-8e84-50d3-8caf-75c51cb5c866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30bb13ff-8e84-50d3-8caf-75c51cb5c866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317713} :answer-field/id #uuid "942bc8de-494e-59d4-aef2-b5344825d601" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318749}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18f346cd-4181-53c0-a9f8-c1ee4d65ecf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-350528" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(x+3)^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(x+3)^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{u}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(x+3)^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{u}^{8}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }2{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}2{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-3}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{27}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc93f73c-03ee-59bf-93ed-905ccaef63cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{72}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc93f73c-03ee-59bf-93ed-905ccaef63cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{72}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318304} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f24bd57-1f42-5b26-8e2a-3e8bcc7e1d43" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318696}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d8204e3-36f5-5613-9381-17a7588e2a05" :question/math-academy-id "q-350530" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{(3y+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=3y+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $y\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3y+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{(3y+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{3{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{(3y+2)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{3{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{3}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{5}{3}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{5}{9}{u}^{-3}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-3}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{9}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b355d9a6-e99e-5ef7-91c3-31ee7ef8c4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b355d9a6-e99e-5ef7-91c3-31ee7ef8c4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318171} :answer-field/id #uuid "a16a7e76-2e13-5b78-a1c4-a4fc2dd41d9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318778}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7437525-50e8-5c3a-901b-813c72ad9b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-350531" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(4t+3)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=4t+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4t+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(4t+3)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(4t+3)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-3{u}^{-1/2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b108f43-8cc9-5ed2-849e-bf0b3ad9c65c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b108f43-8cc9-5ed2-849e-bf0b3ad9c65c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317813} :answer-field/id #uuid "c422af80-a92f-5eec-bc99-8a36d0097520" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318846}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab199904-ad8d-5246-bdfd-890bb0a93820" :question/math-academy-id "q-350532" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=5\\theta +1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $\\theta \\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5\\theta +1$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\theta $ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "325cede2-6960-5085-95d8-87d16b121023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "325cede2-6960-5085-95d8-87d16b121023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319547} :answer-field/id #uuid "85532c92-78b7-5603-b881-ac2fb0559928" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320436}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4fafe02-cb7d-53b6-a541-a5c4876489aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-350534" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{1/2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{1/2}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e197f9-926d-5a38-b07b-3b834b50535f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369820} {:answer/id #uuid "dafbddd2-29ab-58ca-9736-d248a9876ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369821} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7cd5c5-dc32-5789-8e7c-5560c7a38366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369822} {:answer/id #uuid "6db6a9c1-e6da-5b48-a604-1d3d52519aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369823} {:answer/id #uuid "990bf603-9c44-51da-bab2-c8dd72364300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "990bf603-9c44-51da-bab2-c8dd72364300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369824} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc0112dd-0650-59f6-9f8d-f8fe5b3a18d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369819}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f9f79e8-4c67-5d89-b4b2-6ab9cd147e21" :question/math-academy-id "q-152053" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(1+{x}^{3})}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(1+{x}^{3})}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(1+{x}^{3})}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "703e41a4-7d89-5c9f-bced-f0d638d7838b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "703e41a4-7d89-5c9f-bced-f0d638d7838b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370623} :answer-field/id #uuid "edbc9163-b55c-5bce-840b-693a8baab0a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370622}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33e3355d-955a-5955-a1cd-54387f01dc2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-346591" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(x+1)^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(x+1)^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(x+1)^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{7}{u}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{7/2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{7/2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0acdd02-a3ba-5daf-84fc-f0e64c6786e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0acdd02-a3ba-5daf-84fc-f0e64c6786e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370632} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8dbcc38-1fb6-5581-b775-a5c110cb46fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370631}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae3b907d-af45-56d7-b164-a879ab5fe0cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-350533" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{1/2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{1/2}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f416306-21bf-539b-9b9c-b3c0c7c1874f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f416306-21bf-539b-9b9c-b3c0c7c1874f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377537} :answer-field/id #uuid "482395fb-c7fb-5370-bf50-f7fa1aec416c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377536}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3bc676ae-5eea-5c37-8152-babd81d7b946" :question/math-academy-id "q-350536" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{10{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{5}+1)^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{5}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{5}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{10{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{5}+1)^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{u}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{10{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{5}+1)^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{u}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }2{u}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}2{u}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{4}{7}{u}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{7/2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{7/2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13aeb1d6-3a6d-5d19-bcaa-cfdc021ab9c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494352} {:answer/id #uuid "695c0ea1-77ec-56ce-a589-ce5482b85acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494532} {:answer/id #uuid "b03dad7b-0e07-5cb2-b401-99a18df64f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494730} {:answer/id #uuid "e02b37dc-ebae-57b4-af31-87e189cd4c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494848} {:answer/id #uuid "ed16ccaa-3b79-5f36-a20f-c1259e6c3bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b03dad7b-0e07-5cb2-b401-99a18df64f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494730} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc8e448d-ca79-54b8-bab8-a1334af95399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495014}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3a99356-f2d9-5da7-917f-fe8d47cb0308" :question/math-academy-id "q-152780" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3t-2)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=3t-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3t-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3t-2)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3t-2)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{-3/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-3/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{(a)^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{(4)^{3/2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}]}
